Apple this week <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/08/06/apple-account-card-in-wallet-now-available-in-more-countries/">expanded support</a> for its Apple Account Card in the Wallet app to Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, and Malta. The Apple Account Card option for Wallet <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2022/05/25/apple-account-card-wallet-app/">started rolling out in 2022</a>, and Apple has been gradually expanding the feature to more countries since then.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/08/apple-account-card.jpeg" alt="" width="1920" height="1080" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1058803" /><br/>
<h2>What is the Apple Account Card?</h2><br/>
So what is it, exactly? The card displays the balance associated with a user's Apple Account (formerly Apple ID), and it can be used to make purchases in any brick-and-mortar Apple Store and online at Apple.com. It can also be used to buy apps in the App Store, as well as subscriptions and other Apple services.<br/>
<br/>
It's also where the credit goes if you use it to redeem an Apple Gift Card. An Apple Account Card can also be topped up using a linked debit or credit card (via the "Add Money" option in Wallet). <br/>
<br/>
<h2>It's NOT an Apple Credit Card</h2><br/>
Note that an Apple Account Card is different from Apple Card, the latter of which is a straightforward digital and physical credit card for making financial transactions at contactless payment terminals (via Apple Pay), as well as in stores, restaurants, and other locations where Apple Pay isn't yet accepted. <br/>
<br/>
Comparatively, an Apple Account Card is a digital-only card for money available in your Apple Account balance. There's no credit line, no interest, and no rewards attached. Neither is there a physical version of the Apple Account Card. You can't send money to a friend with it, and the balance isn't something you can cash out.<br/>
<br/>
